Martin Rex <mrex@sap.com> writes:=0A=
=0A=
>There is a concept called "provable correctness", =0A=
=0A=
The problem with provable whatever is that it merely proves that, as far as=
=0A=
the provers can tell, the thing they're dealing with conforms to some abstr=
act=0A=
model.  I don't think you can prove much about whatever hiding the ContentT=
ype=0A=
is supposed to achieve because there's no model for it that says, for examp=
le,=0A=
"for an attacker with these capabilities, under these conditions, the=0A=
following security guarantees are provided".=0A=
=0A=
However, we do have a pile of empirical data showing that pretty much any=
=0A=
seems-like-a-good-idea traffic-hiding really only works until the moment=0A=
someone tries to attack it.  The best reference for this is "Peek-a-Boo, I=
=0A=
Still See You: Why Efficient Traffic Analysis Countermeasures Fail" by Dyer=
 et=0A=
al.  So at the moment I'd say that if there's some measure that's completel=
y=0A=
free (no downsides for anything else) then you may as well apply it because=
 it=0A=
can't make things any worse, but not to say "let's do X because it seems li=
ke=0A=
a good idea" when it has no empirically-demonstrable benefit but lots of=0A=
drawbacks.=0A=
=0A=
And it's the "empirically-demonstrable" that's important, Peek-a-Boo is cho=
ck=0A=
full of examples of things that seemed like a good idea but that don't=0A=
actually provide much, if any, benefit, while at the same time introducing =
all=0A=
sorts of downsides.=0A=
=0A=
Peter.=

